          "text": "The bug is a missing exit in `case 3`. If a three-character string does not start with `'y'` or `'Y'`, execution falls through into `case 4`. Inputs such as `\"tru\"` can then cause a `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException` when accessing `charAt(3)`.\n\nReturn `false` when the three-character string does not match `\"yes\"`:\n\n```java\npublic static boolean toBoolean(String str) {\n    if (str == \"true\") {\n        return true;\n    }\n    if (str == null) {\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    switch (str.length()) {\n        case 2: {\n            char ch0 = str.charAt(0);\n            char ch1 = str.charAt(1);\n            return\n                (ch0 == 'o' || ch0 == 'O') &&\n                (ch1 == 'n' || ch1 == 'N');\n        }\n        case 3: {\n            char ch = str.charAt(0);\n            if (ch == 'y' || ch == 'Y') {\n                return\n                    (str.charAt(1) == 'e' || str.charAt(1) == 'E') &&\n                    (str.charAt(2) == 's' || str.charAt(2) == 'S');\n            }\n            return false; // Prevent fall-through into case 4.\n        }\n        case 4: {\n            char ch = str.charAt(0);\n            if (ch == 't' || ch == 'T') {\n                return\n                    (str.charAt(1) == 'r' || str.charAt(1) == 'R') &&\n                    (str.charAt(2) == 'u' || str.charAt(2) == 'U') &&\n                    (str.charAt(3) == 'e' || str.charAt(3) == 'E');\n            }\n            return false;\n        }\n        default:\n            return false;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `str == \"true\"` comparison is a valid fast path here: strings that are not the same object are still checked by their characters afterward."
